        <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-white rounded-lg p-6 shadow">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-lg mb-2">Why is copper wire theft so common?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Copper theft has surged due to record-high copper prices (roughly $4.50+ per pound). A thief's scrap payout is small next to the damage left behind: cut wiring, torn-out walls, and rework. Construction sites are prime targets with exposed wiring and materials.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-white rounded-lg p-6 shadow">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-lg mb-2">How much does copper theft cost construction projects?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Far more than the copper is worth. On top of replacing the wire, you pay for rewiring labor, repairs to walls and equipment damaged during the theft, schedule delays, and overtime to catch up. Use the calculator on this page to estimate the exposure on your own site.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-white rounded-lg p-6 shadow">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-lg mb-2">How do surveillance systems prevent copper theft?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Surveillance trailers let you watch material storage areas 24/7 and send instant alerts to your phone or computer when there's after-hours activity. The footage clearly identifies thieves, and visible cameras discourage a lot of opportunistic theft.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-white rounded-lg p-6 shadow">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-lg mb-2">When are construction sites most vulnerable to copper theft?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Weekends and holidays, when sites sit unattended, are high-risk windows. Thieves also target sites during construction phases when walls are open and wiring is exposed but not yet energized.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-white rounded-lg p-6 shadow">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-lg mb-2">Can stolen copper be tracked or recovered?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Rarely. Once stolen copper is stripped and melted down at scrap yards, it's practically impossible to trace. Prevention through surveillance is the only effective strategy.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-white rounded-lg p-6 shadow">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-lg mb-2">What are Washington's scrap metal laws for copper?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Washington State RCW 19.290 requires scrap metal dealers to record seller identification for every copper transaction, hold purchased copper for a mandatory 5-business-day waiting period before processing, and report all transactions to a law enforcement database. Dealers who buy copper without following these requirements face penalties including license revocation. These laws make it harder for thieves to sell stolen copper, but surveillance remains essential because many thefts are fenced through out-of-state yards or unlicensed buyers.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-white rounded-lg p-6 shadow">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-lg mb-2">Can surveillance footage help prosecute copper thieves in WA?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Yes. Our surveillance systems create court-ready evidence packages that Washington prosecutors use to secure convictions. Footage includes timestamped HD video of the theft, license plate captures linked to scrap yard transaction records, and thermal imaging showing wire-cutting activity. When paired with RCW 19.290 scrap dealer records, prosecutors can trace stolen copper from jobsite to scrap yard.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
